# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTES (not part of the diff -- read before applying)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. This assumes `compile()` itself already accepts a `verify` parameter
#    (compile_optional_verify.patch). If it doesn't yet, apply that patch
#    first -- this one alone is not enough for phase3_v4's
#    `inspect.signature(compile_for_hardware).parameters` check to find
#    `verify` and start passing verify=False.
#
# 2. Default stays True (no behavior change for existing callers of
#    compile_for_hardware() that don't pass verify explicitly) -- same
#    backward-compatibility stance as compile_optional_verify.patch itself.
#
# 3. Once this is applied, re-run phase3_v4_dense_pair_blocks.py. Its
#    startup line will flip from
#        "WARNING: this installed compile_for_hardware() does NOT expose
#        a `verify` parameter yet"
#    to
#        "compile_for_hardware() supports `verify` -- measuring PSF-Zero
#        with verify=False"
#    -- that's the confirmation the fix landed. Compare the resulting
#    PSF-Zero compile times against this run's (verify=True, unpatched)
#    times to see how much of the current slowdown vs. Qiskit was the
#    self-check, same as section 4's own verify=True -> verify=False
#    story.
#
# 4. This does NOT touch the gate-count/depth compression PSF-Zero already
#    shows in this run (~6.7x fewer 2-qubit gates, ~4-5.6x shallower depth
#    at every size tested) -- verify only affects the self-check step
#    inside synthesize(), not the synthesis result itself. That part of
#    the finding stands regardless of this patch.
